### requestsAll.rest:

### Unified VS Code REST Client Test Suite — FullStackOpen Part 13 (Chapter 4)
### Run requests sequentially from top to bottom using "Send Request".
###
### Pre-requisite:
###   1. Fresh clean seed before starting: run `npm run seed` in terminal.
###      Seeded users:
###        - barrybee@yahoo.com / secret (owns the 3 seeded blogs)
###        - deanbug@yahoo.com  / secret (second user for 403 ownership tests)
###   2. Start the server: `npm start` (runs migrations and listens on port 3001).
### ============================================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3001/api
@healthUrl = http://localhost:3001

# Seeded & test credentials
@user1Email = barrybee@yahoo.com
@user2Email = deanbug@yahoo.com
@password = secret

# Automatic response captures via VS Code REST Client (`# @name ...`).
# Re-running a tagged request updates the corresponding variable automatically.
@user1Token = {{loginUser1.response.body.token}}
@user2Token = {{loginUser2.response.body.token}}
@user1Id = {{createUser1.response.body.id}}
@user2Id = {{createUser2.response.body.id}}
@createdBlogId = {{createBlogUser1.response.body.id}}
@readingListId1 = {{addReadingList1.response.body.id}}
@readingListId2 = {{addReadingList2.response.body.id}}


### ============================================================================
### 1. HEALTH CHECK & DATABASE RESET
### ============================================================================

### 1.1 Health check -> Expected: 200 OK ("OK")
GET {{healthUrl}}/

### 1.2 Get all users (Confirm Seeding).
GET {{baseUrl}}/users


### 1.3 Reset database tables -> Expected: 204 No Content
### Clears users, blogs, reading_lists, and sessions so tests start deterministically.
POST {{baseUrl}}/reset


### ============================================================================
### 2. USER CREATION, PASSWORD HASH EXCLUSION & VALIDATION
### ============================================================================

### 2.1 Create User 1 -> Expected: 201 Created
### Verifies `passwordHash` is omitted via `User.findByPk` + `defaultScope`, and `disabled` defaults to false.
# @name createUser1
POST {{baseUrl}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{user1Email}}",
  "name": "Barry Bee",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 2.2 Create User 2 -> Expected: 201 Created
# @name createUser2
POST {{baseUrl}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{user2Email}}",
  "name": "Dean Bug",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 2.3 Invalid username validation -> Expected: 400 Bad Request
### `username` uses `isEmail` validation; `errorHandler` maps Sequelize errors to 400.
POST {{baseUrl}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "not-an-email",
  "name": "Invalid Email User",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 2.4 Duplicate username -> Expected: 400 Bad Request
### Unique constraint on `users.username` triggers a Sequelize error caught as 400.
POST {{baseUrl}}/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{user1Email}}",
  "name": "Duplicate Barry",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 2.5 List all users -> Expected: 200 OK
### Confirms `passwordHash` never appears on any user object.
GET {{baseUrl}}/users


### 2.6 Update user's name by username -> Expected: 200 OK
### Uses `userFindByUsername` middleware and saves updated `name`.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/users/{{user1Email}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Barry B. Bee"
}


### 2.7 Update non-existent user -> Expected: 404 Not Found
PUT {{baseUrl}}/users/nonexistent@example.com
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Ghostly User"
}


### ============================================================================
### 3. LOGIN & SESSION CREATION (EXERCISE-25)
### ============================================================================

### 3.1 Login User 1 -> Expected: 200 OK with { token, username, name }
### Uses `User.unscoped()` internally to verify password, then inserts a row into `sessions`.
### Check in to psql: psql postgres://postgres:root@localhost:5432/blog_db
### and run: `SELECT id, user_id, LEFT(token, 20) AS token_prefix FROM sessions;`
### or: `SELECT * FROM sessions;`, to show full web token.
# @name loginUser1
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{user1Email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 3.2 Login User 2 -> Expected: 200 OK with { token, username, name }
# @name loginUser2
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{user2Email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### 3.3 Login with wrong password -> Expected: 401 Unauthorized ("invalid username or password")
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "{{user1Email}}",
  "password": "wrongpassword"
}


### 3.4 Login with unknown user -> Expected: 401 Unauthorized ("invalid username or password")
POST {{baseUrl}}/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "nobody@example.com",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### ============================================================================
### 4. BLOGS: AUTHENTICATION, CRUD, SEARCH, LIKES & OWNERSHIP
### ============================================================================

### 4.1 Create blog without token -> Expected: 401 Unauthorized ("token missing")
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Unauthorized Blog",
  "author": "Anonymous",
  "url": "https://example.com/unauth",
  "likes": 1,
  "year": 2024
}


### 4.2 Create blog with malformed token -> Expected: 401 Unauthorized ("token invalid")
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer invalid.jwt.token

{
  "title": "Bad Token Blog",
  "author": "Anonymous",
  "url": "https://example.com/bad-token",
  "likes": 1,
  "year": 2024
}


### 4.3 Create Blog A as User 1 -> Expected: 201 Created
### Links blog to `req.decodedToken.id` (User 1).
# @name createBlogUser1
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{user1Token}}

{
  "title": "Test Blog Alpha Created",
  "author": "Barry Bee",
  "url": "https://example.com/test-blog-Alpha",
  "likes": 15,
  "year": 2024
}


### 4.4 Create Blog B as User 1 -> Expected: 201 Created
# @name createBlogUser1Second
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{user1Token}}

{
  "title": "Test Blog Beta Created",
  "author": "Dean Bug",
  "url": "https://example.com/test-blog-Beta",
  "likes": 25,
  "year": 2025
}


### 4.5 Create blog with invalid year (< 1991) -> Expected: 400 Bad Request
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{user1Token}}

{
  "title": "Too Old Blog",
  "author": "Barry Bee",
  "url": "https://example.com/too-old",
  "likes": 0,
  "year": 1990
}


### 4.6 Get all blogs (ordered by likes DESC, includes user without passwordHash) -> Expected: 200 OK
GET {{baseUrl}}/blogs


### 4.7 Search blogs case-insensitively by title or author -> Expected: 200 OK
### Matches "React Patterns and Hooks" (title) and "Dean Bug" (author).
GET {{baseUrl}}/blogs?search=Beta


### 4.8 Update likes on Blog A -> Expected: 200 OK
PUT {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{createdBlogId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "likes": 30
}


### 4.9 Update likes on non-existent blog -> Expected: 404 Not Found
PUT {{baseUrl}}/blogs/999999
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "likes": 10
}


### 4.10 Non-owner (User 2) tries to delete User 1's blog -> Expected: 403 Forbidden
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{createdBlogId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{user2Token}}


### 4.11 Owner (User 1) deletes own blog -> Expected: 204 No Content
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{createdBlogId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{user1Token}}


